{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall make its dishes</b> Rashi explained (Rashi on Exodus 25:29) that קְעָרֹתָיו is its molds, וְכַפּוֹתָיו are the bowls in which they place the frankincense, and וּקְשׂוֹתָיו are like half-tubes, and מְנַקִּיֹּתָיו are the split supports that support the tubes. And these vessels are likewise mentioned in the Gemara (Menachot 97a). And the Rav said that the expression 'מְכִילָתֵהּ' means 'its bearers,' from the language of 'וְנִלְאֵיתִי כַּלְכֵל' (Jeremiah 20:9), 'נִלְאֵיתִי הָכִיל' (ibid. 6:11). And the Sages of Israel disagreed concerning it (in Menachot ibid.), for some say: קְשׂוֹתָיו — these are the supports, and מְנַקִּיֹּתָיו — these are the tubes, but Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 25:29), who translated 'מְכִילָתֵהּ,' was following the view of the one who says מְנַקִּיּוֹת — these are the supports. These are his words, may his memory be blessed.\n\nBut this did not sit well with me, because מְכִילָתֵהּ in Aramaic is nothing but a term for measures, like אֵיפוֹת (ephods / dry measures) and similar things, for thus the Targum translates 'אֵיפַת צֶדֶק' (Leviticus 19:36) — 'מְכִילָן דִּקְשׁוֹט,' 'לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ בְּבֵיתְךָ אֵיפָה וְאֵיפָה' (Deuteronomy 25:14) — 'לֹא יְהֵא לָךְ בְּבֵיתָךְ מְכִילְתָא וּמְכִילְתָא,' and so too everywhere in the Talmud and in the words of the Sages: כַּיֵּל מֵיכַל, בִּמְכִילְתָא דְּכָיֵל אִנֵּשׁ בַּהּ מִתְּכִיל (Jerusalem Targum on Genesis 38:26), and likewise 'נִלְאֵתִי הָכִיל' (Jeremiah 6:11) — an expression of a measure that holds, from 'אַלְפַּיִם בַּת יָכִיל' (I Kings 7:26), 'וְכָל בַּשָּׁלִישׁ' (Isaiah 40:12), 'קָטֹן מֵהָכִיל אֶת הָעֹלָה' (I Kings 8:64), and so many others. And likewise 'נִלְאֵתִי כַּלְכֵל' (Jeremiah 20:9), in my opinion, is not an expression of bearing, but rather is from this very root: 'I am weary of containing,' like the expression 'לֹא תוּכַל הָאָרֶץ לְהָכִיל אֶת כָּל דְּבָרָיו' (Amos 7:10), and likewise 'רוּחַ אִישׁ יְכַלְכֵּל מַחֲלֵהוּ' (Proverbs 18:14) — that he can contain it within himself and not loathe it, 'הִנֵּה הַשָּׁמַיִם וּשְׁמֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם לֹא יְכַלְכְּלוּךָ' (I Kings 8:27) — they cannot contain and hold the magnitude of Your loftiness, for You have no limit or measure, 'אַף כִּי הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה' the small one 'אֲשֶׁר בָּנִיתִי' (ibid.), 'יְכַלְכֵּל דְּבָרָיו בְּמִשְׁפָּט' (Psalms 112:5) — that a fool should not pour out his entire spirit, but contain his words within himself as fitting and bring them forth as fitting, 'וַיְכַלְכֵּל יוֹסֵף' (Genesis 47:12) — that he gave them food as much as they could contain, neither overabundant nor lacking, but bread according to the number of children, and so all of them.\n\nBut the intent of Onkelos is not clear. Perhaps מְנַקִּיֹּתָיו in his view is a term for a measure, for there was a measure holding two tenths (of an ephah) of flour to measure the one חַלָּה (loaf) with it; they would not measure with the עִשָּׂרוֹן used for meal-offerings, and he does not hold by the Mishnah that teaches 'two dry measures were in the Sanctuary: the עִשָּׂרוֹן and the half-עִשָּׂרוֹן' (Menachot 87a), but in his view there were three measures there: the עִשָּׂרוֹן, the half-עִשָּׂרוֹן, and the two-עֶשְׂרוֹנִים. But what seems correct in my eyes regarding his view is that the מְכִילָא is the mold made for shaping the dough, as they said (ibid. 94a): 'There were three molds there; she places it in the mold while it is still dough, and a kind of mold was in the oven, and when he removes it he places it in the mold so it should not be spoiled.' And this mold, whose purpose is so it should not be spoiled, does not need to be in the shape and size of the bread, but is made like a kind of bowl to support the walls of the bread, and it is קְעָרֹתָיו. But the first mold, for the dough, is made according to the measure and size of the bread — its length ten (handbreadths), its width five, and its corners seven fingerbreadths — and in it they set the dough and measure it and shape it, and therefore it is called מְכִילָא, because it is made for a measure.\n\nAnd the expression 'מְנַקִּיּוֹת' is a noun, like אֵיפָה and סְאָה, just as קְעָרֹתָיו and קְשׂוֹתָיו and the rest of the names that do not have a descriptive form. And perhaps the justified measures called אֵיפַת צֶדֶק and הִין צֶדֶק would be called מְנַקִּיּוֹת, because they are clean (נְקִיּוֹת) from falsehood and cleanse (מְנַקּוֹת) their owners from fraud and sin. And הַקְּשָׂוֹת is a comprehensive term including the tubes and the supports, or perhaps because of their being split they are called thus by interchange of צ/דִ\"י with שׁ/י\"ן, like 'וַיִּירְאוּ יֹשְׁבֵי קְצָוֹת מֵאוֹתֹתֶיךָ' (Psalms 65:9), as an interchange of צָדִ\"י and שִׁי\"ן in the word for laughter.\n\nAnd R. Abraham ibn Ezra wrote (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 25:29): 'There is an error in Chronicles (I Chronicles 28:17), for it mentioned מִזְרָקוֹת in place of קְעָרֹת, and כְּפוֹרִים in place of הַכַּפּוֹת, and it mentioned הַקְּשָׂווֹת as they are, and מִזְלָגוֹת in place of the מְנַקִּיּוֹת, and all of gold for the needs of the table; or perhaps they were other vessels that David commanded to place on the tables that Solomon his son would make, and such was not the table of the Tent of Meeting.' But the error is in the words of Rabbi Abraham, for what the verse says — 'וְהַמִּזְלָגוֹת וְהַמִּזְרָקוֹת וְהַקְּשָׂוֹת זָהָב טָהוֹר וְלִכְפוֹרֵי הַזָּהָב בְּמִשְׁקָל לִכְפוֹר וּכְפוֹר' — is not connected only with 'לְשֻׁלְחֲנוֹת הַמַּעֲרֶכֶת' (I Chronicles 28:16), but rather refers back to what the verse said (ibid. in verses 13–14): 'וּלְכָל מְלֶאכֶת עֲבוֹדַת בֵּית ה\\\"י וּלְכָל כְּלֵי עֲבוֹדַת בֵּית ה\\\"י,' and it said: 'לַזָּהָב לְכָל כְּלֵי עֲבוֹדָה וַעֲבוֹדָה.' And it mentioned the מִזְלָגוֹת and מִזְרָקוֹת, which are vessels of the altar, and the קְשָׂווֹת for the table, and the כְּפוֹרִים for the altar, and it mentioned (ibid. v. 18) 'וּלְמִזְבַּח הַקְּטֹרֶת זָהָב מְזֻקָּק בַּמִּשְׁקָל,' and it said (ibid. v. 19): 'הַכֹּל בִּכְתָב מִיַּד ה\\\"י עָלַי הִשְׂכִּיל כֹּל מַלְאֲכוֹת הַתַּבְנִית' — and behold this is a general statement encompassing all the needs of the Holy Temple."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "In the final paragraph, Ramban quotes I Chronicles verses; the abbreviation ביהמ\"ק (בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ) is rendered 'the Holy Temple.' The divine name abbreviation ה\\\"י in quoted verses is rendered HaShem. Ramban's interjection against Ibn Ezra is structured as a long syntactic refutation; the clause 'והנה הוא כלל' closes the argument by reclassifying the Chronicles passage as a comprehensive heading for the entire Temple, not the table alone."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:28.463909706Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}